    {
      "id": "279b2497-2531-4ac0-b40f-3e356eb65062",
      "type": "user_prompt",
      "ts": "2026-07-08T20:43:45.512Z",
      "attrs": {
        "text": "You are helping an engineering lead document a new system. The following skill is active for this task — follow it exactly:\n\n---BEGIN SKILL \"writing-design-docs\"---\nA design doc is a decision record for a reader who wasn't in the room. It has this shape:\n\n## Required shape, in order\n1. **Nutshell first.** Section 1 states the proposed solution in well under a page: what it is, the 2–3 load-bearing decisions, the headline cost/latency numbers, and the main overview diagram. A reader who stops after section 1 knows the design.\n2. **Terms and notation defined upfront**, before first use. Define anything not universally known (p95, ev/s, at-least-once, idempotent…). No unexplained jargon anywhere in the doc.\n3. **Main architecture and data flow next.** Deep dives, alternatives, and details after.\n4. **Every major decision gets an alternatives table**: Option | Pros | Cons | Verdict — chosen option in the first row, and a \"when we'd switch\" note per rejected option.\n5. **Back-of-the-envelope section, always.** Assumptions in their own table first, each with its basis. Then one-significant-figure arithmetic for data volume, storage, CPU/memory, latency, and money — show the multiplication so it's checkable. End with a per-unit conclusion a buyer understands (\"~1¢/dev/day\", \"fits one Postgres\").\n6. **Clarifying questions table.** Never block on unanswered questions: Question | Working assumption used in this doc. Flag any assumption that contradicts an earlier doc.\n7. **Phasing section last**: v0/v1/v2 and the named seams where deferred pieces slot in.\n\n## Diagrams\n- **Mermaid only — no ASCII art.** `flowchart` for block diagrams and data flow, `stateDiagram-v2` for lifecycles, `erDiagram` for data models.\n- **Validate every block, then look at it.** Extract each block, render with `npx @mermaid-js/mermaid-cli` to PNG, and visually inspect the image. Fix what you see:\n  - edges attaching to subgraph borders instead of nodes → remove `direction` overrides from subgraphs that have cross-boundary edges;\n  - overlapping labels → shorten labels, move detail to prose, drop self-loops;\n  - stages rendering out of order → declare the main chain's edges first.\n\n## Style rules\n- Terse and compact. Selective about content, but what's included is complete sentences — no fragments or arrow-chain shorthand.\n- **No code in architecture docs** — prose, tables, diagrams only. Table/entity names are fine; SQL, schemas-as-code, and snippets are not.\n- Libraries: recommend only widely used, actively maintained open source. Name rejected candidates and why (archived, commercial, single-maintainer).\n- Report limits honestly: unknowns, unclassified residue, and accuracy caveats are stated in the doc, not hidden.\n---END SKILL---\n\nTask: create a design document at /tmp/skilltest/green-doc.md for a new internal service: a feature-flag service for ~40 internal applications (flag definitions, per-environment targeting rules, client SDK polling). Decide the architecture yourself; keep the document under ~150 lines. This is a documentation-only task — do not write any code files, do not set up a project. When the file is written and diagrams are validated, reply with a one-line summary of what you validated.",
